  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 142 PM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nThe long term period starts out mild and quiet as we remain on the \nwestern periphery of a longwave ridge, placing us in a southwesterly \nflow regime. As a result, high temperatures on both Wednesday and \nThursday will peak 10-15 degrees above climatology (average high for \nCheyenne on Thursday is 57F). The southwesterly flow will allow for \na surge of mid to upper-level moisture from Baja California ahead of \nan amplified closed low located off the coast of California. Latest \nNAEFS guidance suggests PWAT near the climatological maximum on \nThursday (~0.75\"), which is quite remarkable given that our \nsouthwestern monsoon pattern typically ends in early to mid \nSeptember. The main question will be if we can actually translate \nany of this rich moisture down to the surface as precipitation, \nwhich has been difficult to do so far this year. Unfortunately, our \nprospects aren't exactly looking favorable for any widespread QPF as \nthe main synoptic-scale forcing for ascent is temporally offset from \nthe primary moisture plume. The main DCVA combined with weak \nisentropic ascent looks to be over SE Wyoming sometime on Friday, \nwhich also looks like the day with our best chance for showers. \nThunderstorm chances look to be low at this time as LREF guidance \nand forecast soundings show MLCAPE generally below 100 J/Kg, with \nslightly higher values in the Panhandle. Friday also looks to be \nquite mild, with median high temperatures per LREF guidance staying \nin the mid 60s to mid 70s east of the I-25 corridor, and primarily \n50s west of the Laramie Range. \n\nThe unsettled pattern will continue into the weekend as the closed \nlow gradually translates to the east over the Desert Southwest, \nplacing us in a region of weak zonal flow aloft. Again, not overly \nenthusiastic about chances for widespread precipitation across SE \nWyoming and the Nebraska Panhandle given that forcing for ascent is \nrather weak. Still quite a ways out so not going to get into \nspecifics, but deterministic runs are showing a cold front moving \nacross our region some time on Saturday with a 10-15 degree drop in \nhigh temperatures relative to Thursday and Friday, so we may \nactually see an \"average\" day of fall weather this weekend with \nchances for precipitation, though shower coverage will likely be \nspotty.",
